    "justification": "This is an all-tests information bound, so it survives arbitrary higher-order correction and establishes a genuine nuisance cost.",
    "gap": "Kennedy et al. provide a scalar CATE fuzzy bound; the exact Hilbert null, embedded signal, and covariance-source constraint are new here.",
    "consumer": "It tells analysts when no regularization or nuisance learner can restore oracle sensitivity in observational conditional-distribution testing.",
    "proof_tex": "Choose dyadic \\(h_n\\) within a fixed factor of \\(c_hn^{-2/(2t+d)}\\). Since \\(t<d/2\\), \\(K_{h_n}\\gtrsim n\\), so the sparse-occupancy premise holds under uniform design. The legality lemma shows that every component satisfies all fixed model conditions for large \\(n\\), including the covariance profile and, for \\(0<s\\le1/2\\), the source constraint. The cell lemma gives\n\\[\nP_\\omega\\in\\mathcal M_0,\\qquad\n\\|\\Psi(Q_\\omega)\\|\n\\ge ca_{h_n}b_{h_n}\n=cc_\\circ^2h_n^t\n\\ge c'n^{-2t/(2t+d)}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\nThe source norm is \\(O(a_{h_n}^2b_{h_n}^2)=o(1)\\), hence is below \\(R^2\\); every \\(Q_\\omega\\) is in the separated alternative.\n\nThe mixture bound gives\n\\[\nH^2(\\overline Q_{h_n}^{(n)},\\overline P_{h_n}^{(n)})\n\\le Cn^2h_n^{2t+d}=Cc_h^{2t+d}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\nChoose \\(c_h\\) so the right side makes\n\\(\\operatorname{TV}(\\overline Q_{h_n}^{(n)},\\overline P_{h_n}^{(n)})<1-\\alpha-\\beta\\), using \\(\\operatorname{TV}\\le\\sqrt{2H^2}\\). If a test had the required errors for every component, averaging over \\(\\omega\\) would give\n\\[\n1-\\alpha-\\beta\n\\le E_{\\overline Q_{h_n}^{(n)}}\\varphi_n\n -E_{\\overline P_{h_n}^{(n)}}\\varphi_n\n\\le\\operatorname{TV}(\\overline Q_{h_n}^{(n)},\\overline P_{h_n}^{(n)}),\n\\]\na contradiction. Hence\n\\[\n\\delta_n^\\star\\ge c'n^{-2t/(2t+d)}.\n\\]\nThe explicit constant-contrast path used in the oracle proof is admitted here: uniform design and \\(e=1/2\\) obey the design, overlap, and propensity conditions; positive interior slack admits its sufficiently small constant arm perturbations; its covariance differs from the baseline Fourier covariance only in the positive constant eigenvalue; and its source norm tends to zero for every fixed \\(s\\). Thus the narrowed oracle theorem gives \\(\\delta_{n,e}^\\star\\asymp n^{-1/2}\\). Since\n\\[\n\\frac12-\\frac{2t}{2t+d}=\\frac{d-2t}{2(2t+d)}>0,\n\\]\nthe ratio diverges.",
